const e="NEIS Center · Allied Industrial Base Explorer",t={noCountryMakesEverything:"No country needs to make everything.",whatNotToBuild:"A strong industrial strategy is as much about deciding what not to build as deciding what to build.",partnerIsAStrategy:"Relying on trusted partners, or on open and diversified supply, is a legitimate strategic choice, not a failure to build at home."},a={compacts:"Sectoral Compacts are the delivery mechanism. They bring together the smallest useful group of countries around a specific bottleneck and define what each partner contributes, what each receives, and what action closes the remaining gap."},i="The Allied Industrial Base is a strategy for managed interdependence. No country should try to make everything. The goal is to identify the capabilities that must be secure, decide which trusted partners are best placed to provide them, and align trade, investment, finance and demand so that specialization produces both resilience and scale.",n="Build what matters. Specialize where allies are strongest. Keep enough redundancy that no single supplier can hold the system at risk.",o="The aim is an allied network that is harder to coerce, cheaper than everyone duplicating everything, and strong enough to compete at scale.",s={supply:{question:"Who has the strongest capability?"},demand:{question:"Which countries need a partner?"}},r={home:"Where should countries build, partner and rely on allies?",sector:"Where are the real bottlenecks and opportunities?",sectorLanding:"What matters in this supply chain?",sectorProfile:"Who is best placed to do this, and what makes the step strategic?",concentration:"Where are the chokepoints?",country:"What should this country secure, build, partner for or deploy?",aib:"How should allies divide the work?",coverage:"Who can cover each supply-chain step?",coordination:"Where do allies need to act together?",partnerships:"Who should work with whom, and for what?",compacts:"Which bottlenecks justify a Sectoral Compact?",roles:"What should each country contribute?",methods:"How the Explorer moves from evidence to a strategic choice"},c={node:"supply-chain step",nodeClass:"what this step needs",compactUnnecessary:"no compact needed",fragmentedAction:"today's uncoordinated response",supplyAssumption:"assumption about how much suppliers can expand",bestPlacedPartner:"Best-placed partner",postureClassification:"What should this country do?"},l={secure:"Secure",preserve:"Preserve",catch_up:"Catch Up",partner:"Partner",accelerate:"Accelerate",watch:"Watch"},d={secure:"Protect what cannot be allowed to fail",preserve:"Keep and strengthen an existing strength",catch_up:"Build from a credible industrial base",partner:"Rely on trusted partners or diversified supply",accelerate:"Deploy faster at home",watch:"Keep open: buy competitively, do not force localization"},h={secure:"Protect an acute vulnerability. The answer may be domestic capacity, trusted allied supply, stockpiles, substitution or redundancy, not necessarily self-sufficiency.",preserve:"Keep and strengthen a capability the country already does well and that still has strategic value.",catch_up:"Build capability where the strategic prize is high and the country has a credible base to build from. Catch Up means there is something real to build on, not a greenfield ambition.",partner:"The capability matters, but duplicating it at home is not the best answer. Rely on trusted partners or diversified supply, and make that reliance durable.",accelerate:"Deploy a technology faster because the domestic energy system needs more of it. It says nothing about making the equipment at home.",watch:"Do not make domestic production a major policy priority. Keep access affordable and diversified, and watch for changes in risk or opportunity."},p={catch_up:"catch up in",partner:"rely on partners for"},u="Low domestic priority",m="Deployment need is below the bar for acceleration: the current demand, target and system evidence do not call for a major push.",y="Partner / Diversify",g={small:"Small yard",medium:"Medium yard",large:"Large yard"},b={high:"High fence",medium:"Medium fence",low:"Low fence"},w={small:"Small yard / High fence",medium:"Medium yard / medium, permeable fence",large:"Large yard / Low fence"},E={small:"Capabilities where foreign control or concentrated dependence could create an unacceptable security or resilience risk.",medium:"Capabilities worth building or preserving because they shape competitiveness, industrial learning or future technological leadership.",large:"Capabilities where affordable and diversified access generally matters more than domestic production."},f={small:"Secure",medium:"Preserve, Catch Up, Partner, and Accelerate at Deployment & Use",large:"Watch"},v={high:"High fence · Secure",medium:"Medium fence · Build, preserve, partner and deploy",low:"Low fence · Keep open"},A={high:"Protect the capabilities where dependence could create an unacceptable security or resilience risk.",medium:"Focus industrial policy where productive capability has strategic value, and use allies where specialization is the better answer.",low:"Do not spend scarce industrial-policy effort where domestic production adds little strategic value."},I={high:"High-fence capabilities are deliberately narrow: control systems, genuine chokepoints and other supply-chain steps where disruption would be hard to absorb quickly. The answer is not always reshoring: trusted allied supply, stockpiles, substitution and redundancy can also solve the problem.",medium:"The medium fence is about competitiveness, learning and future capability. A country may preserve an existing strength, catch up where it has a credible base, rely on a partner where duplication makes little sense, or accelerate deployment where the energy system needs the technology.",low:"Low-fence capabilities are not unimportant. They are places where affordable, diversified access generally matters more than domestic production. The right strategy may be to buy competitively, keep suppliers diversified and focus policy elsewhere."},N="A technology can sit in more than one yard. Its supply-chain steps, and the layers within a step (control software, firmware, a critical input), can each need a different fence.",T="A control layer is in the Small yard only where the data show coercive foreign control for this country.",_="A control layer is in the Small yard only where the data show coercive foreign control for that economy.",C="A control layer is in the Small yard only for a country where the data show coercive foreign control.",S="The three yards are different policy problems, not low, medium and high scores on one scale.",k="The strategy starts with evidence, not policy. Need asks whether dependence or deployment creates a real problem. Prize asks whether productive capability has strategic industrial value. Position asks how strong the country already is. Those three determine the choice. Only then do we compare the recommendation with what government is actually doing.",O=["need","prize","position","choice","policy"],R={need:{name:"Need",measure:"Strategic Need",question:"What problem has to be solved?",plain:"At producing stages, Need means supply-chain vulnerability. At Deployment & Use, it means how urgently the domestic energy system needs more deployment."},prize:{name:"Prize",measure:"Strategic Industrial Value",question:"Is productive capability here strategically valuable?",plain:"Market growth matters, but so do process learning, supplier ecosystems and future leadership."},position:{name:"Position",measure:"Competitive Position",question:"How much real capability does the country have today?",plain:"Exports and specialization, production, capacity, investment, costs and resources where they apply."},choice:{name:"Choice",measure:"Strategic choice",question:"What should the country do?",plain:"Secure, Preserve, Catch Up, Partner, Accelerate or Watch (keep open)."},policy:{name:"Policy alignment",measure:"Policy alignment",question:"Is policy focused on the right places?",plain:"Is government action matched to the problem, or using the wrong amount or type of intervention?"}},L={supplyChainNeed:"Supply-chain vulnerability",deploymentNeed:"Deployment need"},M={supplyChainNeed:"How vulnerable a country is at a producing-stage capability: import dependence, supplier concentration, exposure to risky suppliers and the availability of trusted alternatives.",deploymentNeed:"How urgently a country needs more of a technology in use, based on expected demand, national targets and energy-system requirements.",prize:"How much strategic value there is in building or keeping productive capability here. It combines the size of the market with learning, supplier ecosystems and future leadership potential, not just market growth.",competitivePosition:"How strong the country is today: exports and specialization, production, capacity, investment, costs and resources where they apply."},P={aligned:"Broadly aligned",under_prioritized:"Needs more attention",over_prioritized:"Too much intervention",misaligned:"Wrong tool for the problem",mixed:"Mixed signals",insufficient_data:"Not enough policy evidence"},D="Needs more attention: deployment policy lags",W="Is policy focused on the right places?",B="The strategy is derived without using current policy. This section then asks whether government action matches the recommended choice, or is spending effort in the wrong place.",H={watchHeavyProtection:"Policy is trying to localize a capability the strategy does not treat as a domestic priority.",partnerLocalization:"The need is real, but policy is trying to solve it through domestic duplication rather than trusted specialization.",accelerateWeakPolicy:"The energy system needs faster deployment than current policy appears to support."},z={operational_control:"Control-system risk",coercive_chokepoint:"Chokepoint risk",systemic_resilience:"System resilience"},x={COORDINATE:"Coordinate trade",BUILD:"Build capacity","MANAGE SPECIALIZATION":"Manage specialization","LOW INTERVENTION":"Low intervention","INSUFFICIENT DATA":"Not enough evidence"},F={COORDINATE:"Existing supplier capacity is enough, but countries need new or expanded trade relationships to use it.",BUILD:"Even the best coordinated trade response leaves a material gap. New production or processing capacity is required.","MANAGE SPECIALIZATION":"Specialization creates economic value, but too much supply sits with one supplier. Keep the specialization while adding enough redundancy to reduce the risk.","LOW INTERVENTION":"The existing network is already resilient enough that major coordination is unlikely to add much.","INSUFFICIENT DATA":"The data are too thin to make a confident recommendation."},G="What each supply-chain step needs",U={baseline:"Today's network",managed:"Managed Interdependence",decoupling:"Major-supplier disruption"},Y={baseline:"Baseline",managed:"Managed Interdependence",decoupling:"Decoupling stress test"},q={baseline:"The suppliers and trade relationships that exist today. No disruption is imposed.",decoupling:"A major supplier (China, in this analysis) stops supplying allied markets. The analysis asks what existing partners can replace, what coordinated trade adds, and what still requires new capacity."},j="How resilient is the network under different assumptions?",K={question:"This is the core AIB question: how much specialization can allies keep while reducing the dependencies that create coercive or systemic risk?",explainer:"Managed Interdependence is the alternative to both laissez-faire concentration and blanket decoupling. It keeps the benefits of specialization but sets limits where one supplier's position creates unacceptable risk. The question is not whether dependence can be eliminated, but which dependencies are worth keeping, and what backup is enough."},V={exposure:"Supply exposed",dividend:"Gain from acting together",residual:"Remaining gap"},X={alliedDemandAffected:"Demand that must find a new supplier",kept:"Still supplied",restoredByTrade:"Recovered through trade",newCapacity:"Requires new capacity",leftForNewCapacity:"Gap that still needs new capacity",whereLostSupplySits:"Where the remaining vulnerability sits"},Z={coverage:"The share of allied demand that the available supplier network can meet under a given scenario.",coordinationDividend:"The extra demand allies can meet when they coordinate trade rather than relying only on today's bilateral relationships.",remainingGap:"Demand that cannot be met even after the best available coordinated trade response. This is where new capacity may be needed.",minimumCoalition:"The smallest group of partners that can meet the compact's resilience target while keeping the main buyer markets inside the agreement.",n1Resilience:"How much demand the network can still meet if its largest remaining supplier is lost.",concentrationCap:"A limit on how much of allied demand one supplier may serve in the Managed Interdependence scenario. Supply above the cap has to shift to alternatives."},Q={definition:"A Sectoral Compact is a practical bargain among the countries needed to solve a shared supply-chain problem. It identifies the bottleneck, the smallest useful group of partners, the role each partner should play, and the mix of trade, investment and specialization needed to close the gap.",notAClub:"A compact is not a club of every friendly country. It is a targeted agreement around a specific value chain where coordination produces a measurable benefit over countries acting alone. It should be no larger than necessary, and every member should have a reason to take part.",reciprocity:"The point is reciprocal specialization: partners accept some dependence on one another because each gets reliable access, a meaningful economic role, and a more resilient market in return.",strategic:"The longer-run division of labor for a strategically important value chain: which countries supply, buy, invest, finance or host capacity.",sectionTitles:{why:"Why this compact?",what:"What does the compact cover?",together:"What can the partners solve together?",next:"What should governments do next?"}},J="Importance to the compact",$={lede:"A resilient allied system depends on countries doing different things well. This view shows what each economy contributes to the network (supply, demand, technology, capital or new capacity) and where its role is essential rather than interchangeable.",key:"The goal is not to reward the country with the most roles. It is to make specialization explicit enough that partners know what they are relying on one another for.",derived:"Roles are derived from the trade, capacity and compact results. They describe what a country contributes to a specific value chain, not a permanent geopolitical label."},ee={lede:"A good partnership is not simply a friendly relationship. It should solve a specific strategic problem: reduce a dangerous dependency, reinforce a competitive capability, open a market, or help build capacity where the allied network needs it.",heading:"Which partners are best placed to help?",thesisHeading:"What is the strategic case for this partnership?",thesisPrompt:"Is the relationship mainly about securing supply, expanding a market, building new capacity, or combining complementary strengths? The answer should determine the tools.",psiCaveat:"Use the component scores to understand the reason. A high partnership score is not a recommendation to cooperate on every technology."},te="The strategy is built from the latest common data available. Trade uses a structural benchmark plus a more recent momentum signal where reporting is complete enough to compare countries fairly.",ae="Evidence & methodology",ie="See the data behind each choice, where evidence is thin, and how the security and opportunity scores read the same sectors.",ne="Security and opportunity scores",oe="These scores describe relative security and opportunity: how one country compares with other economies in each sector. They are a descriptive view beside the strategic choices, which are the recommendation.",se={notAssessed:"Not assessed: the available data are not strong enough to make a strategic call.",notAssessedShort:"Not assessed",noStrategyMap:"There is not enough comparable evidence to produce a strategy map for this country.",aibLoading:"Loading the Allied Industrial Base results…",aibUnavailable:"Allied Industrial Base results are not available in this build. Country and sector evidence are still available.",noAibRoleWhy:"That does not mean it is unimportant; it means the current stress test does not require its capability to close the identified gaps.",noCoalition:"No coalition is needed under this scenario: the identified supply problem is already covered or does not meet the compact threshold.",weakProductionEvidence:"Production evidence is weak",dataLimitation:"Missing or thin data are shown as uncertainty, never as a low priority."};export{q as $,i as A,V as B,C,v as D,ie as E,t as F,W as G,ae as H,te as I,p as J,K,oe as L,r as M,Q as N,Z as O,e as P,F as Q,b as R,ne as S,c as T,z as U,D as V,u as W,d as X,S as Y,H as Z,g as _,k as a,Y as a0,j as a1,X as a2,$ as a3,a as a4,G as a5,J as a6,O as b,R as c,w as d,E as e,f,N as g,o as h,n as i,s as j,L as k,y as l,se as m,B as n,P as o,T as p,h as q,m as r,l as s,ee as t,x as u,U as v,I as w,A as x,M as y,_ as z};
